<section id="philosophy" class="h-screen snap-start">
    <div class="flex h-full w-full">
        <div class="w-1/5"></div>
        <div class="flex w-1/5 items-center"></div>

        <div class="flex h-full w-2/5 items-center justify-start pt-[7vh] pb-[5vh] pl-[2vw]">
            <div data-about-copy class="max-w-[34vw]">
                <h2 class="mt-[1vh] text-[4.2vh] font-semibold tracking-tight text-white">
                    My Philosophy
                </h2>
                <p class="text-[1.1vh] uppercase tracking-[0.45em] text-white/40">
                    On Building Things
                </p>

                <div
                    class="mt-[1vh] space-y-[2.8vh] text-[1.5vh] leading-[1.9] text-white/70"
                >
                    <p>
                        I think the best engineers treat software the same way a craftsman 
                        treats his work. Every project is another chance to create something 
                        beautiful, the only way to learn is to make mistakes. And every 
                        problem has a dozen different ways to be approached, and sometimes, 
                        to get something wrong feels just as good as getting something right, 
                        in fact, sometimes I try to get it as wrong as humanly possible, 
                        theres fun in seeing a chaos.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        One of the best things that can happen to you in this field is to find
                        someone who loves and respects the craft as much as you do. Talent and
                        skill is obviously a great thing, but to admire the field, even in its
                        goofyness, is a rare and wonderful feeling. The most fun projects
                        tend to start off with a <span class="text-white italic">"I know this 
                        sounds weird but what if-"</span> and the best projects are 
                        ones where you dont know what final product you're aiming for, after 
                        all, the journey is just as fun as the destination.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The goofier the project, the better.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        This field puts you into predicaments that perhaps nobody in the history of
                        mankind has ever dealt with before, there are no right answers, but there are 
                        fun approaches, and who knows? maybe someday I myself will get to set the 
                        standard for what is right and what is wrong, but until then, I will 
                        continue to make mistakes, and learn from them.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        That's why I'm incredibly proud of every major project
                        I've built. I could probably spend hours or days talking about why <a 
                        href="https://drive.anga.codes/" class="text-blue-500 hover:text-blue-600">
                            AngaDrive
                        </a> is efficient down to the last CPU clock
                        cycle, or why I designed my <a 
                        href="https://compiler.anga.codes/" class="text-blue-500 hover:text-blue-600">
                            Online Compiler
                        </a> the way I
                        did. After all, every optimization, every redesign, and every mistake
                        has a story behind it.
                    </p>

                    <blockquote
                        class="border-l-2 border-white/15 pl-6 text-white/90 italic"
                    >
                        A project to an engineer is what jewelry is to a goldsmith.
                        Anyone can appriciate the beauty of the craft, but only
                        the maker can truly admire the process by which it was forged.
                    </blockquote>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="w-1/5"></div>
    </div>
